From d98c661198f454c992e53ae6a3a395bccd9f755c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: papi <papi@papi-ux.com>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 07:44:40 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 7/7] feat(video): negotiate PyroWave in HDR10, and read the
 profile as a list

A host that can carry both colourimetries offers one token for each, in one fmtp
line, space separated. So the matcher stops requiring the whole value to equal
its token and looks for its token as a whole element of the list instead. That is
the change that lets a host offer a second token without breaking every client
that knows only the first, and it keeps the property the whole value match was
there for: a whole element is still not a prefix, so a client that knows "-v1"
does not accept a host offering "-v10".

Two formats above the two that were there, filling the nibble the PyroWave mask
already reserved: 4:2:0 and 4:4:4 at ten bits. They join VIDEO_FORMAT_MASK_10BIT,
which is what makes the SDP generator send dynamicRangeMode 1 for them with no
second mechanism, and that field is how a host learns a stream is HDR. A ten bit
format that stayed out of that mask would negotiate as SDR and be decoded as
Rec. 709.

Both ends have to have it, as with the chroma, and for a sharper reason: the
chroma travels in every frame's sequence header so a decoder built for the wrong
one refuses the lot, while the colourimetry travels in neither, so a mismatch
there is a picture that is merely wrong. SCM_PYROWAVE_HDR10 is what a client
reads to know, because the dynamic range is asked for at launch over HTTP, before
any of this.

The SDP text logic moves into PyroWaveSdp.h. It is the only protocol decision this
client makes by parsing text, and it had no test: the harness it was written
against was a throwaway in a temp directory and is gone. As a header it can be
included by one that lives in the repository.
